// Test simfile with hand-worked durations and footings, for calibrating ddr-trainer's chart
// analyzer (src/chart_analyzer.rs), whose tests check the values below. Generated by
// test_resources/make_test_simfiles.py, so edit that instead of this.
//
// One BPM (120, so quarter notes are 0.5 s apart and 8ths 0.25 s), no stops.
//   - the duration runs from the chart's first object (a note, mine or hold end) to its last,
//     and the active ratio is the fraction of it between notes at most 1 s apart, or holding
//   - the notes are split into segments at gaps longer than 2 s, unless a hold is still
//     down at the gap's end. The foot simulation starts
//     each segment with the feet at home (left foot on Left, right on Right) and gives it
//     the cheapest footing by its costs. The tests use fixed ones (TEST_COSTS in
//     src/chart_analyzer/footing.rs), where an event costs its base plus its speed cost
//     times how fast the notes are (1 for notes 0.125 s apart, so 0.25 for quarter notes
//     here and 0.5 for 8ths): a horizontal crossover 4 + 1 x speed, a doublestep
//     1 + 7 x speed, a bracket 6 + 1 x speed, a full spin 16 + 4 x speed
//   - each event and pattern found has its own speed in notes per second: an event's is 1
//     over the gap since the note before, a pattern's its notes less one over its seconds.
//     So 2 for quarter notes here and 4 for 8ths
//
//   chart      duration  active  segments  doublesteps  brackets  staircases  gallops
//   Beginner   0         0       none      0            0         0           0
//   Easy       7         1/2     5 1       0            0         1 at 2      0
//   Medium     5.5       4/11    3 3       2 at 2       0         0           0
//   Hard       4.75      6/19    4 4       0            0         0           0
//   Challenge  10        7/20    6 4 6     2 at 4       1 at 4    0           1 at 4
//
// Segments are in notes. Every other event and pattern count is 0 in every chart.
// Each note costs what its step's events do, so 0 for most. Per note, over the chart:
//   Medium: 2 doublesteps of 2.75 on 6 notes: mean 5.5/6, median 0, max 2.75
//   Challenge: 2 doublesteps of 4.5 and a bracket of 6.5 on 16 notes: mean 15.5/16, median 0,
//   max 6.5. Nothing costs anything in the other charts.
// Cost per second: per note, the costs of the notes from it to less than 1 s later.
//   Medium: each doublestep is the third of three notes 0.5 s apart, so in the windows of
//   the second and third: 0, 2.75, 2.75, twice. Mean 11/6, median 2.75, max 2.75
//   Challenge: the doublesteps are on the third and fifth of the first six notes, 0.25 s
//   apart: 4.5, 9, 9, 4.5, 4.5, 0. The bracket is on the third of the last six, the first
//   four 0.25 s apart: 6.5, 6.5, 6.5, 0, 0, 0. With 4 notes of 0 between: mean 51/16,
//   median (0 + 4.5)/2, max 9
// Easy (duration): 2 s to 9 s (a mine), active from 2 s to 5.5 s (quarter notes, then a
//   hold). Left, Down, Up, Right is a staircase, however slow.
// Medium (breaks reset): Left, Down, Right in quarter notes, twice, 3.5 s apart. Alternating
//   would cross the left foot over to Right (4.25). The right foot stepping Down then Right is
//   a doublestep (2.75), which is cheaper this slow. The feet go home in the break.
// Hard (either foot starts): Up, Left, Up, Left crosses over if the left foot starts, so the
//   right foot does, and after a break Up, Right, Up, Right crosses over if the right foot
//   starts, so the left does.
// Challenge (realistic mix): Left, Down, Right, Up, Left, Down in 8ths alternates into a
//   crossover and a full spin (22.5). Two doublesteps (4.5 each) get round the pad without
//   turning. Then Up, Left, Up, Left as in Hard. Then a hold on Left with Down, Up, Down under
//   it: the right foot would doublestep twice (9), so the left foot taps Up while it holds
//   Left, a bracket (6.5), and the right foot stays on Down. The last two notes, Up and Right
//   0.25 s apart and 0.5 s after the note before, are a gallop.
// A foot's move is a step after an earlier one of its own in the segment: its speed is 1 over
// the seconds between, its distance is across the pad (2 from Left to Right, sqrt(2) between
// neighbours) and its velocity is the one times the other.
//   Easy: the left foot goes Left, Up, Left and the right Down, Right, each 1 s after its
//     last step: 3 moves of sqrt(2) at speed 1
//   Medium: the doublestepping right foot goes Down to Right in 0.5 s, twice: 2 moves of
//     sqrt(2) at speed 2, velocity 2 sqrt(2). The left foot steps once a segment: no move.
#TITLE:Footing Stats;
#ARTIST:ddr-trainer;
#MUSIC:footing_stats.ogg;
#OFFSET:0.000000;
#BPMS:0.000=120.000;
#STOPS:;

//---------------dance-single - nothing in it----------------
#NOTES:
     dance-single:
     nothing in it:
     Beginner:
     1:
     0.412,0.356,0.250,0.100,0.000:
// measure 0: empty
0000
0000
0000
0000
,  // measure 1: empty
0000
0000
0000
0000
;

